WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.490
Make in batches, and price each piece so it pays you.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.020
This is the video for module two of Handmade Accessories for Sale.

3
00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:17.080
In this module you sew a lined tote, and make bracelets, earrings and headbands. The secret to doing it well is batching.

4
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.330
A written recipe card keeps every piece the same.

5
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:29.050
Many makers price by guessing, or copy the cheapest seller. Then they work for weeks and earn almost nothing.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.200
Costing fixes that. You know exactly what one piece costs before you name a price.

7
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:44.840
Making one pair of earrings from start to finish, then the next, means picking up the same tools again and again. Pieces come out different.

8
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:53.360
Batching means doing one step for all pieces before you move on. Glue dries while you work, and every piece matches.

9
00:00:54.610 --> 00:01:07.010
For example, Ada in Enugu used to take about 25 minutes a pair. On a Saturday she tries a batch of ten pairs. First she cuts all 20 circles, choosing matching motifs in pairs.

10
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.920
Then she presses all 20 buttons with her kit.

11
00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:22.220
Next she opens 20 jump rings. Twist the ends sideways, one towards you and one away. Never pull a ring open into an oval. That weakens the metal.

12
00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.030
Then she attaches the buttons to the ear wires.

13
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:36.540
Last, she checks every pair and puts it on a card. Ten pairs took about 115 minutes, roughly 12 minutes a pair instead of 25.

14
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:47.540
A price has four parts. Materials are everything in one piece, including packaging and labels. Divide bulk prices by how many pieces they make.

15
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:55.620
Labour. Your time is a cost. Multiply a fair hourly rate by the time per piece from your batch timings.

16
00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:06.590
Overhead is the cost of running the business: fuel or electricity, needles, data and transport. Add up a month, and divide by the pieces made.

17
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.690
Profit is money for the business itself, to buy stock, replace tools and grow.

18
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.840
Cost is materials plus labour plus overhead. Price is cost plus profit.

19
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:25.440
Let us cost a pair of Ada's earrings. These prices are approximate examples. Use your own receipts.

20
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:34.760
Two cover buttons, ear wires, jump rings, fabric, glue, a card and a small bag come to about 1,020 naira.

21
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:42.520
Her labour is 12 minutes a pair at 1,500 naira an hour. That is 300 naira.

22
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:50.740
Her overhead is about 6,000 naira a month across about 60 pieces. That is 100 naira a pair.

23
00:02:51.990 --> 00:03:00.740
So one pair costs her 1,420 naira. Adding about 50 percent profit gives about 2,130.

24
00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:06.530
She prices the pair at 2,500 naira, within the range of similar sellers.

25
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:15.820
A formula is the start, not the end. Compare your price with what your buyer already pays. If it is close, you are in range.

26
00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:25.010
If yours is well above, show what makes it better, simplify the design, batch to save time, or target buyers who pay more.

27
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:36.990
If the market price is below your cost, do not drop your price. Every sale below cost makes you poorer, even if money is coming in. Change the product, or drop it.

28
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.590
Keep every receipt. Your costing is only as accurate as your records.

29
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:52.560
Payment links, POS charges and platform fees take a little from each sale. Check your provider's current fees, and build them into your price.

30
00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.340
Agree with the buyer in writing who pays for delivery before you send anything.

31
00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:07.930
Material prices change often. Recheck them every time you restock, and at least every three months. And never sell below cost.

32
00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:16.260
To remember. Batch one step across many pieces, and follow your recipe card. Open jump rings sideways.

33
00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:23.420
Cost is materials plus labour plus overhead. Price is cost plus profit. And never sell below cost.

34
00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:32.150
Now sew and check one lined tote, run your first batch of at least six pieces, and cost and price two of your products.

35
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:38.990
The lessons below show the full tote method and more worked examples. Continue with the lessons below.
